# WireMesh controller configuration (systemd EnvironmentFile).
# The controller is entirely env-configured. Defaults shown; uncomment to change.
# The controller's OWN state dir (CA key/cert, the SQLite DB, secrets/) — the
# FRESH-INSTALL default, deliberately split from the gateway's
# /var/lib/wiremesh so the two can share a host: that directory belongs to the
# gateway (root-owned 0700, and the gateway unit drops CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE), so a
# controller running as `wiremesh` there either can't read it or steals it from
# the gateway. The unit's StateDirectory=wiremesh-controller creates this path
# owned wiremesh:wiremesh.
#
# UPGRADING from a release that defaulted to /var/lib/wiremesh? Your state
# STAYS THERE and the package pins this line back to /var/lib/wiremesh for you.
# Nothing is ever moved automatically — a maintainer script cannot safely
# relocate a live controller's SQLite DB, and getting it half-done used to mean
# a regenerated CA and a re-enrollment of the whole fabric. Splitting a
# co-located host is a deliberate, service-down procedure: see docs/install.md.
#
# Why the pin is needed at all: BOTH package formats replace this file on
# upgrade when you have not edited it. deb has no conffile "noreplace"
# mechanism at all, and rpm's %config(noreplace) only protects a file whose
# checksum differs from the one the old package shipped. So on an unmodified
# config, deb and rpm alike silently install this new version — pointing at
# the new directory — and the postinstall then puts your path back.
#
# CONSEQUENCE OF THE PIN, worth knowing: once the postinstall has edited this
# file, the package manager considers it locally modified.
#   - deb: the next release that changes this file will PROMPT you
#     ("Modified (by you or by a script) since installation").
#   - rpm: the next release that changes this file will NOT touch yours; it
#     writes /etc/wiremesh/controller.env.rpmnew instead, SILENTLY. New
#     settings we ship will not reach you until you diff that file in. If you
#     run rpm hosts, check for *.rpmnew after every wiremesh-controller
#     upgrade.
WIREMESH_DATA_DIR=/var/lib/wiremesh-controller
# Bind enroll/sync/observe to all interfaces so remote gateways can reach them.
# (Admin TCP is ALWAYS loopback-only regardless of this.)
WIREMESH_BIND_IP=0.0.0.0
# WIREMESH_TCP_PORT=9400          # enrollment (server-TLS)
# WIREMESH_SYNC_TCP_PORT=9500     # Sync (mTLS)
# WIREMESH_OBSERVE_UDP_PORT=9600  # endpoint observation
# WIREMESH_ADMIN_TCP_PORT=9443    # admin (loopback-only)
#
# AUTOMATIC KEY ROTATION IS OFF UNLESS YOU SET AN INTERVAL HERE. Leaving this
# line commented out means NO rotation timer runs at all — not a 30-day one.
# (Earlier releases treated the unset variable as "30d"; that default is gone.
# Note where the change lives: this LINE was commented out before and after, so
# nothing you configured moved — the behaviour changes when the new controller
# BINARY starts. But the comment block around it IS rewritten by this release,
# which makes it a change to this file, so the rules above apply. If your copy
# is locally modified — the postinstall pin makes it so on any host upgraded
# from /var/lib/wiremesh, and so does any edit of your own — deb PROMPTS and
# rpm writes controller.env.rpmnew and keeps yours. On rpm that leaves you
# holding the OLD block, the one that still says the default is 30d: it is now
# wrong, and the correction exists only in the .rpmnew. MERGE IT. If your copy
# is untouched, both formats simply install this new version. Either way, if
# you were relying on the old default the upgrade silently stops rotating and
# you have to write the interval below to get it back.)
#
# Arming the timer is an INFORMED CHOICE, because the thing it drives has a
# known-open defect: backlog item 9, the rotation wedge. A gateway honours a
# rotate directive only while its rotation state machine is idle, and a
# rotation that fails part-way through parks that state machine off-idle. Such
# a gateway then SILENTLY IGNORES every later rotation until its process is
# restarted, and never scrubs the old private key — the security half of the
# rotation does not happen. Do not count on being told: the gateway logs
# `ROTATION WEDGED` for exactly ONE route — a cutover whose watch set empties —
# and the most reachable route, a rotation that fails part-way through, logs
# nothing at all. That line also lands in the GATEWAY's journal
# (journalctl -u wiremesh-gateway), not this controller's, so watching the
# controller after you arm the timer shows you nothing. The scheduled timer is
# what turns that into a fabric-wide event, because it fires for every active
# gateway at once: the v0.7.0 `off` escape hatch was shipped to defuse exactly
# that, a fabric-wide rotation outage scheduled for the first timer fire.
# See docs/BACKLOG.md item 9 and docs/install.md.
#
# The supported way to replace a key you believe is compromised is MANUAL
# rotation — `fabricctl` / the Admin RotateKey RPC, one gateway at a time,
# watched. That path is unaffected by anything on this line.
#
# If you still want a schedule: <integer><s|m|h|d>, lowercase unit.
# WIREMESH_ROTATION_INTERVAL=30d
#
# A malformed value is a STARTUP ERROR — the controller refuses to boot rather
# than quietly ignoring what you wrote. `0d`/`0s` is likewise rejected: use
# `off`. So is anything above 3650d (10 years) — a timer that never fires is
# `off` without the boot warning that says so.
#
# `off` is the explicit spelling of the default. Write it to record that the
# absence of a timer is deliberate rather than an oversight:
# WIREMESH_ROTATION_INTERVAL=off
#
# With no timer — unset or `off` — rotation is not disabled as a CAPABILITY:
#   - rotations already in flight still complete (the controller's decision
#     sweep keeps running, and still recovers a rotation orphaned by a crash);
#   - manual rotation on demand still works (`fabricctl`/Admin RotateKey) — use
#     it if you need to replace a key you believe is compromised;
#   - nothing else changes: keys already in use keep working indefinitely.
# What does NOT happen is scheduled rotation: no key is rotated on a timer, for
# as long as the controller runs. The controller prints a loud warning at every
# boot saying so — on a stock install that banner is now the expected state,
# not a misconfiguration. Changing this takes effect on controller restart.
